/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/isc2-cc.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("isc2-cc", [["ic1",0,1,"A hospital must ensure patient records are not changed without authorization. Which security principle is the main concern?",["Confidentiality","Availability","Integrity","Non-repudiation"],2,"Integrity protects data from unauthorized or accidental modification.","Outline Domain 1",["Confidentiality is about keeping records from being seen by unauthorized people, not about preventing changes to them.","Availability ensures records can be reached when needed; it does not stop unauthorized modification.",null,"Non-repudiation proves who performed an action so they cannot deny it; it does not by itself prevent records from being altered."],2],["ic2",0,1,"In AAA, what does accounting provide?",["A record of what a user did so actions can be traced","Proof of who the user is","A decision about what the user may access","Encryption of the user's session"],0,"Accounting logs user activity for auditing and tracing; authentication proves identity and authorization grants access.","Outline Domain 1",[null,"Proving who the user is describes authentication, the step before any activity is recorded.","Deciding what a user may access is authorization, not the logging of what they actually did.","Encrypting the session protects confidentiality in transit and is not one of the AAA functions."],1],["ic3",0,1,"A company buys cyber insurance to cover losses from a data breach. Which risk treatment is this?",["Avoidance","Mitigation","Acceptance","Transference"],3,"Insurance shifts the financial impact of the risk to another party, which is transference.","Outline Domain 1",["Avoidance means stopping the risky activity altogether; buying insurance keeps the activity and only shifts the financial loss.","Mitigation applies controls to reduce likelihood or impact; insurance does not make a breach less likely or less damaging, it just pays for it.","Acceptance means knowingly bearing the risk yourself, whereas insurance passes the cost to the insurer.",null],2],["ic4",0,1,"Which of the following is an administrative (managerial) control?",["Firewall","Acceptable use policy","Door lock","Disk encryption"],1,"Policies are administrative controls; firewalls and encryption are technical, and locks are physical.","Outline Domain 1",["A firewall is a technical (logical) control that filters traffic, not a management-level rule or process.",null,"A door lock is a physical control that restricts access to a space.","Disk encryption is a technical control implemented in software or hardware, not an administrative policy."],1],["ic5",0,1,"An ISC2 member learns that a colleague is falsely claiming to hold the CC certification. What is the best action?",["Ignore it because it is not their business","Report it through ISC2's ethics complaint process","Post the colleague's name on social media","Ask the colleague to share their exam questions"],1,"The Code of Ethics calls for acting honorably and advancing the profession, which includes reporting misrepresentation through proper channels.","Outline Domain 1",["The ISC2 Code of Ethics expects members to protect the profession, so ignoring known misrepresentation of a credential is not acceptable.",null,"Publicly shaming someone online is not a proper channel and could itself be unprofessional or defamatory.","Asking for exam questions would violate the exam's confidentiality agreement and does nothing about the false claim."],3],["ic6",0,1,"Which term describes the combination of the likelihood of a threat exploiting a weakness and the impact if it does?",["Vulnerability","Threat","Asset","Risk"],3,"Risk is measured by likelihood and impact.","Outline Domain 1",["A vulnerability is only the weakness itself; it does not combine likelihood and impact.","A threat is the potential cause of harm (such as an attacker); it is one input to risk, not the combination described.","An asset is the thing of value being protected, not a measure of likelihood and impact.",null],1],["ic7",0,1,"A user logs in with a password and a fingerprint scan. Which factor types are used?",["Something you know and something you are","Something you have and something you are","Two knowledge factors","Something you know and somewhere you are"],0,"A password is knowledge and a fingerprint is a biometric (inherence) factor.","Outline Domain 1",[null,"A password is not something you have; possession factors are items like a smart card, token or phone.","A fingerprint is a biometric (inherence) factor, so only one of the two is a knowledge factor.","A fingerprint is not a location factor; somewhere you are would be something like GPS or IP-based location."],2],["ic8",0,1,"An unpatched web server that an attacker could exploit is an example of a:",["Threat","Risk","Vulnerability","Control"],2,"A vulnerability is a weakness that a threat could exploit.","Outline Domain 1",["The threat would be the attacker or event that might exploit the server; the unpatched server itself is the weakness.","Risk is the likelihood and impact of the weakness being exploited, not the weakness itself.",null,"A control is a safeguard that reduces risk, such as applying the patch; an unpatched server is the opposite."],1],["ic9",0,1,"Security staff review camera footage to find out who entered a server room after hours. What type of control is the camera acting as?",["Preventive","Deterrent","Detective","Corrective"],2,"Reviewing footage to identify what happened is a detective function.","Outline Domain 1",["A preventive control stops an event before it happens; reviewing footage after the fact does not block anyone from entering.","Visible cameras can deter, but in this scenario the footage is being reviewed to identify who entered, which is detection.",null,"A corrective control fixes or restores something after an incident; identifying the person does not correct anything."],2],["ic10",0,1,"Which technology best provides non-repudiation for an email?",["Digital signature","Symmetric encryption","Unkeyed hashing","Load balancing"],0,"A digital signature made with the sender's private key proves who sent the message, so the sender cannot credibly deny it.","Outline Domain 1",[null,"Symmetric encryption uses a shared key, so either party could have produced the message and neither can prove who sent it.","An unkeyed hash shows whether data changed, but anyone can compute it, so it does not tie the message to a sender.","Load balancing distributes traffic for availability and has nothing to do with proving who sent an email."],2],["ic11",0,1,"Which document states management's high-level security intent and is mandatory?",["Guideline","Procedure","Baseline","Policy"],3,"A policy is the high-level mandatory statement of intent; the other documents support it.","Outline Domain 1",["Guidelines are recommended, optional advice, not mandatory statements of management intent.","Procedures are detailed step-by-step instructions that carry out a policy, not the high-level statement itself.","A baseline defines a minimum security configuration; it is a supporting, technical-level document rather than management's overall intent.",null],1],["ic12",0,1,"A company must protect personal data because the government requires it by law. What is the source of this requirement?",["An internal guideline","Legislation or regulation","An industry best practice","A vendor recommendation"],1,"Requirements imposed by government, such as GDPR, come from laws and regulations and are not optional.","Outline Domain 1",["An internal guideline is written by the company itself and is usually optional, not imposed by government.",null,"Industry best practices are voluntary recommendations, not legal requirements from the government.","A vendor recommendation is advice from a supplier and carries no legal force."],2],["ic13",0,1,"An attacker slips manipulated records into the data used to train a company's AI model. Which principle is mainly threatened?",["Availability","Integrity","Accounting","Non-repudiation"],1,"Data poisoning corrupts the training data, which attacks its integrity and the trustworthiness of the model's output.","Outline Domain 1",["Poisoned training data does not normally make the model unreachable; the problem is that its outputs become untrustworthy.",null,"Accounting is the logging of user actions in AAA, not a property of the training data that poisoning corrupts.","Non-repudiation is about proving who performed an action; slipping in bad records attacks the correctness of the data instead."],2],["ic14",0,1,"Which approach does a qualitative risk assessment use?",["Exact dollar values such as annualized loss expectancy","Only historical breach costs","Hash values of critical files","Ratings such as high, medium and low"],3,"Qualitative assessment ranks risks with descriptive ratings; quantitative assessment uses monetary values.","Outline Domain 1",["Exact dollar values such as ALE are used in quantitative risk assessment, not qualitative.","Relying only on historical breach costs is still a monetary, data-driven approach and is not how qualitative assessment rates risk.","File hashes are integrity checks, not a way of rating risk likelihood or impact.",null],1],["ic15",0,2,"Which measure is designed to warn leadership early that risk exposure is increasing?",["Key risk indicator (KRI)","Recovery point objective","Service level agreement","Mean time to repair"],0,"KRIs are metrics chosen specifically to signal rising risk before it becomes an incident.","Outline Domain 2",[null,"A recovery point objective defines acceptable data loss for recovery planning; it is not an early-warning metric for rising risk.","A service level agreement sets agreed service targets with a provider; it does not signal growing risk exposure.","Mean time to repair measures how quickly failures are fixed after they occur, not whether risk is trending upward."],2],["ic16",0,2,"What is the main purpose of security governance?",["Tuning firewall rules daily","Making users choose strong passwords","Ensuring security activities align with business objectives under leadership oversight","Running nightly backups"],2,"Governance sets direction, accountability and oversight so security supports business goals.","Outline Domain 2",["Daily firewall tuning is an operational task, not the leadership oversight and direction that governance provides.","Enforcing strong passwords is a specific technical or administrative control, not the purpose of governance as a whole.",null,"Running nightly backups is an operational activity that governance may require, but it is not governance itself."],2],["ic17",0,2,"What does an organization's risk appetite describe?",["The number of vulnerabilities found in scans","The cost of a security control","The amount of risk it is willing to accept to pursue its goals","The total value of its assets"],2,"Risk appetite is the level of risk leadership is willing to accept.","Outline Domain 2",["The number of scan findings measures vulnerabilities present, not how much risk leadership is willing to accept.","Control cost is an input to risk treatment decisions, not a statement of risk appetite.",null,"Total asset value helps size potential impact but does not express the organization's willingness to take risk."],1],["ic18",0,2,"Which approach is most effective for building a strong security culture?",["Ongoing awareness training backed by visible leadership support","A one-time onboarding slide deck","Publicly shaming employees who click phishing links","Emailing the policy once a year"],0,"Culture grows from continuous, relevant training and leaders who model the behavior; shaming discourages reporting.","Outline Domain 2",[null,"A one-time onboarding deck is quickly forgotten and does not sustain the behaviors that make up a security culture.","Public shaming discourages employees from reporting mistakes, which undermines the culture instead of strengthening it.","Emailing the policy once a year is passive and infrequent, so it rarely changes day-to-day behavior."],2],["ic19",0,2,"In GRC, what does compliance mean?",["Buying the newest security tools","Never having an incident","Hiring a chief information security officer","Meeting the requirements of applicable laws, regulations, standards and contracts"],3,"Compliance is conformance with external and internal requirements, such as PCI DSS or HIPAA.","Outline Domain 2",["Buying new tools does not by itself show conformance with any law, standard or contract.","Compliance does not guarantee zero incidents; a fully compliant organization can still be breached.","Hiring a CISO may support a security program, but compliance is about meeting requirements, not staffing a role.",null],1],["ic20",0,2,"The click rate on simulated phishing emails falls from 20% to 5% over a year. What does this metric mainly show?",["Email server availability","Effectiveness of the security awareness program","Encryption strength","Patch compliance"],1,"Phishing simulation results are a common measure of how well awareness training is changing behavior.","Outline Domain 2",["Email server availability would be measured by uptime, not by how many users click simulated phishing links.",null,"Phishing click rates reflect user behavior and say nothing about the strength of encryption algorithms.","Patch compliance tracks how many systems have current updates, which is unrelated to users clicking phishing emails."],2],["ic21",0,2,"Who is ultimately accountable for accepting risk in an organization?",["The help desk","Senior management and the board","Individual end users","The external auditor"],1,"Senior leadership owns risk decisions; security teams advise and auditors assess.","Outline Domain 2",["The help desk supports users and follows policy; it has no authority to accept organizational risk.",null,"End users must follow policy but are not accountable for organization-wide risk decisions.","External auditors independently assess controls and must stay independent, so they do not accept risk on the organization's behalf."],2],["ic22",0,2,"Which governance document gives step-by-step instructions for completing a task?",["Policy","Guideline","Baseline","Procedure"],3,"Procedures are detailed, step-by-step instructions that implement policies and standards.","Outline Domain 2",["A policy is a high-level statement of intent and does not give step-by-step instructions.","A guideline offers optional recommendations rather than detailed, required steps.","A baseline specifies a minimum configuration standard, not the sequence of steps to complete a task.",null],1],["ic23",0,2,"A security manager is preparing a report for executives. What should it focus on?",["Risk and program metrics summarized in business terms","Every raw log entry from the SIEM","The full list of firewall rules","Application source code"],0,"Executive reporting should translate security status into business risk so leaders can decide.","Outline Domain 2",[null,"Raw SIEM log entries are far too detailed for executives and do not translate into business risk they can act on.","A full firewall rule list is technical detail for engineers, not a summary executives can use to make decisions.","Application source code is irrelevant to an executive audience and says nothing about overall risk posture."],2],["ic24",0,2,"A new vendor will process the company's customer data. What should happen before the contract is signed?",["Give the vendor full administrator access","Skip review because the vendor is inexpensive","Assess the vendor's security and include security requirements in the contract","Wait until an incident occurs to review the vendor"],2,"Third-party risk management assesses vendors up front and writes security and notification terms into the contract.","Outline Domain 2",["Granting full admin access before any assessment violates least privilege and exposes the company to unvetted risk.","Low cost does not reduce the risk of handling customer data; the vendor still needs a security review.",null,"Waiting for an incident is reactive; by then contract terms and security requirements can no longer be negotiated up front."],2],["ic25",0,3,"Which statement best describes least privilege?",["All IT staff get administrator rights","Everyone gets the same access for simplicity","Users get only the access they need to do their job","Access is granted based on seniority"],2,"Least privilege limits each user to the minimum access their job requires.","Outline Domain 3",["Giving every IT staff member admin rights grants far more access than most roles need, the opposite of least privilege.","Giving everyone identical access ignores job needs and overprivileges most users.",null,"Seniority does not determine need; senior staff often require less system access than specialists."],1],["ic26",0,3,"Which is an example of separation of duties?",["One employee creates vendor payments and a different employee approves them","One administrator holds every password","Team members share one login","A manager approves their own expense report"],0,"Splitting a sensitive task between people prevents one person from committing and hiding fraud.","Outline Domain 3",[null,"One administrator holding every password concentrates control in a single person instead of splitting it.","A shared login destroys individual accountability and does not divide any sensitive task between people.","A manager approving their own expense report is exactly the conflict that separation of duties is meant to prevent."],3],["ic27",0,3,"In which access control model are permissions assigned to job functions and users gain them by being placed in those functions?",["Discretionary access control","Mandatory access control","Time-based access control","Role-based access control"],3,"RBAC grants permissions to roles, and users inherit them through role membership.","Outline Domain 3",["In DAC the resource owner decides who gets access, rather than permissions flowing from job functions.","MAC grants access by comparing system-enforced labels with clearances, not by role membership.","Time-based access control restricts when access is allowed; it does not assign permissions to job functions.",null],1],["ic28",0,3,"A military system grants access by comparing classification labels on data with users' clearances. Which model is this?",["Discretionary access control","Mandatory access control","Role-based access control","Rule-based access control"],1,"MAC is enforced by the system using labels and clearances, not by data owners.","Outline Domain 3",["In DAC the data owner chooses who has access, whereas here the system enforces labels and clearances.",null,"RBAC assigns permissions by job role, not by comparing classification labels with clearances.","Rule-based access control applies administrator-defined rules (such as firewall ACLs), not classification labels matched to clearances."],3],["ic29",0,3,"A user shares a folder and chooses which coworkers can open it. Which model is this?",["Mandatory access control","Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Attribute labels set by the system"],1,"In DAC the owner of a resource decides who can access it.","Outline Domain 3",["In MAC the system enforces access based on labels, and users cannot choose who may open a resource.",null,"In RBAC access comes from job roles set by administrators, not from an owner picking individual coworkers.","Attribute labels set by the system describe a mandatory or attribute-based approach, not an owner choosing who gets access."],2],["ic30",0,3,"An employee is terminated today. What is the most important identity action?",["Reset their password next quarter","Move the account to another department","Leave the account active for handover","Disable or remove their access promptly"],3,"Timely deprovisioning prevents a former employee from using access they no longer should have.","Outline Domain 3",["Waiting until next quarter leaves a terminated employee's access usable for months.","Moving the account to another department keeps it active and still usable by the former employee.","Leaving the account active for handover lets someone who no longer works there keep access; handover should use a manager or shared resources instead.",null],2],["ic31",0,3,"What is privilege creep?",["Users keep accumulating permissions as they change roles","An attacker brute-forces a password","A badge reader fails open during a power cut","A firewall blocks legitimate traffic"],0,"Privilege creep happens when old access is not removed after role changes; access reviews catch it.","Outline Domain 3",[null,"Brute-forcing a password is a credential attack, not the gradual accumulation of permissions.","A badge reader failing open is a physical-security fail-safe behavior, unrelated to permissions building up over time.","A firewall blocking legitimate traffic is a false positive or misconfiguration, not privilege creep."],1],["ic32",0,3,"What is the main purpose of a periodic access review?",["Speeding up user logins","Installing missing patches","Confirming users still need the access they have","Resetting every password"],2,"Access reviews verify that current permissions are still appropriate and remove what is not.","Outline Domain 3",["Access reviews check whether permissions are appropriate; they have no effect on login speed.","Installing patches is vulnerability management, not a review of who has access to what.",null,"Resetting every password changes credentials but does not verify whether each user's access is still needed."],2],["ic33",0,3,"An access control vestibule (mantrap) at a data center entrance mainly prevents:",["Phishing","SQL injection","Tailgating","Malware infections"],2,"A vestibule lets only one authenticated person through at a time, stopping people from following someone in.","Outline Domain 3",["Phishing is an email or messaging attack, which a physical entry vestibule cannot stop.","SQL injection is an application-layer attack on databases, unrelated to controlling who walks through a door.",null,"Malware infections spread through software and networks, not through people following someone into a building."],2],["ic34",0,3,"A user types their username at a login screen. Which step is this?",["Identification","Authentication","Authorization","Accounting"],0,"Claiming an identity is identification; proving it with a password or factor is authentication.","Outline Domain 3",[null,"Authentication is proving the claimed identity, for example by entering a password, which comes after typing the username.","Authorization decides what the user may access after their identity is proven.","Accounting records what the user does after gaining access, not the act of claiming an identity."],1],["ic35",0,3,"What is the main benefit of single sign-on (SSO)?",["It removes the need for authentication","It guarantees no breaches","It eliminates the need for MFA","Users authenticate once to access multiple systems"],3,"SSO lets one authentication grant access to many applications, reducing password fatigue.","Outline Domain 3",["SSO does not remove authentication; the user still authenticates, just once for many systems.","No technology guarantees zero breaches, and a compromised SSO credential can actually expose many systems at once.","SSO does not replace MFA; because one login unlocks many systems, it should be protected with MFA.",null],1],["ic36",0,3,"What is the best practice for system administrators' privileged access?",["Use the admin account for daily email","Use a separate admin account only for admin tasks, protected with MFA","Share one admin account among the team","Turn off logging for admin accounts"],1,"Separate, MFA-protected admin accounts limit exposure and keep admin actions attributable.","Outline Domain 3",["Using an admin account for daily email exposes powerful privileges to phishing and malware.",null,"A shared admin account removes individual accountability, since actions cannot be traced to one person.","Turning off logging for admin accounts hides the most powerful actions from audit and investigation."],3],["ic37",0,4,"Which port does HTTPS use by default?",["80","443","22","25"],1,"HTTPS uses TCP 443; 80 is HTTP, 22 is SSH and 25 is SMTP.","Outline Domain 4",["Port 80 is the default for unencrypted HTTP, not HTTPS.",null,"Port 22 is used by SSH for secure remote shell access, not for HTTPS web traffic.","Port 25 is used by SMTP to send email, not for web traffic."],1],["ic38",0,4,"At which OSI layer does IP routing take place?",["Physical","Data link","Transport","Network"],3,"Routing by IP address happens at Layer 3, the network layer.","Outline Domain 4",["The physical layer (Layer 1) transmits raw bits over media and has no concept of IP addresses.","The data link layer (Layer 2) forwards frames using MAC addresses within a local network, not IP routing.","The transport layer (Layer 4) handles end-to-end delivery with TCP or UDP ports, not routing between networks.",null],1],["ic39",0,4,"Which protocol should replace Telnet for secure remote administration?",["SSH on port 22","FTP on port 21","HTTP on port 80","SNMP on port 161"],0,"SSH encrypts the session, while Telnet sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.","Outline Domain 4",[null,"FTP on port 21 transfers files and sends credentials in clear text, so it is neither secure nor a remote shell.","HTTP on port 80 is unencrypted web traffic and does not provide secure command-line administration.","SNMP on port 161 is for monitoring network devices, and older versions send community strings in clear text."],2],["ic40",0,4,"A company uses a SaaS email service. Which security responsibility remains with the company?",["Physical security of the data center","Patching the provider's hypervisors","Managing user access and the data it stores","Writing the application's code"],2,"Under shared responsibility, SaaS customers still own their identities, access settings and data.","Outline Domain 4",["In SaaS the provider is responsible for physical data center security, not the customer.","The provider patches its own hypervisors and infrastructure; customers have no access to them in SaaS.",null,"In SaaS the provider writes and maintains the application code; the customer only uses the application."],2],["ic41",0,4,"A distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attack mainly targets which principle?",["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability","Non-repudiation"],2,"DDoS floods a service so legitimate users cannot reach it.","Outline Domain 4",["DDoS does not expose data to unauthorized viewers, which is what confidentiality protects.","DDoS floods a service with traffic but does not normally modify data, so integrity is not the main target.",null,"Non-repudiation concerns proving who took an action, which a traffic flood does not attack."],1],["ic42",0,4,"What is the core idea of zero trust?",["Never trust, always verify each access request","Trust every device inside the perimeter","Allow access only through a VPN","Disable logging to improve speed"],0,"Zero trust removes implicit trust based on network location and verifies every request.","Outline Domain 4",[null,"Trusting every device inside the perimeter is the traditional castle-and-moat model that zero trust rejects.","A VPN only establishes a network connection and can still grant broad implicit trust once connected; zero trust verifies each request.","Disabling logging reduces visibility, while zero trust depends on continuous monitoring and verification."],1],["ic43",0,4,"An organization places its smart cameras and other IoT devices on their own VLAN. What is this?",["Load balancing","Port forwarding","Network address translation","Network segmentation to limit lateral movement"],3,"Segmentation isolates less-trusted devices so a compromise cannot easily spread.","Outline Domain 4",["Load balancing spreads traffic across servers for performance and availability; it does not isolate devices.","Port forwarding redirects incoming traffic on a port to an internal host and does nothing to separate device groups.","NAT translates private addresses to public ones; it does not place devices in an isolated segment.",null],2],["ic44",0,4,"What can an intrusion prevention system (IPS) do that an intrusion detection system (IDS) cannot?",["Record suspicious traffic","Block malicious traffic inline","Encrypt network traffic","Assign IP addresses"],1,"An IPS sits in the traffic path and can drop malicious packets; an IDS only alerts.","Outline Domain 4",["Recording suspicious traffic is something an IDS already does, so it is not the IPS's distinguishing capability.",null,"Neither an IDS nor an IPS encrypts traffic; that is the job of protocols such as TLS or IPsec.","Assigning IP addresses is the role of DHCP, not an intrusion detection or prevention system."],2],["ic45",0,4,"Several hospitals share a cloud built for their common regulatory needs. Which deployment model is this?",["Public","Community","Private","Hybrid"],1,"A community cloud is shared by organizations with common concerns such as compliance.","Outline Domain 4",["A public cloud is open to any customer, not built for a specific group with shared regulatory needs.",null,"A private cloud serves a single organization, not several hospitals sharing it.","A hybrid cloud combines two or more deployment models, such as private and public, rather than being shared by a group of similar organizations."],2],["ic46",0,4,"An employee works from coffee shop Wi-Fi. What best protects against man-in-the-middle attacks?",["Choosing a network with a longer name","Turning off the host firewall","Disabling automatic updates","Using a VPN or other encrypted connection"],3,"Encrypting traffic end to end keeps an attacker on the same network from reading or altering it.","Outline Domain 4",["The network name has no bearing on security, and attackers can easily create convincing fake network names.","Turning off the host firewall removes protection and makes the laptop more exposed on a public network.","Disabling automatic updates leaves known vulnerabilities unpatched and does nothing to protect traffic in transit.",null],3],["ic47",0,4,"What is a DMZ (screened subnet)?",["A network segment for public-facing servers, separated from the internal network","A wireless encryption protocol","A type of malware","An alternate recovery site"],0,"A DMZ hosts internet-facing services while firewalls keep them apart from internal systems.","Outline Domain 4",[null,"Wireless encryption protocols are standards like WPA2 or WPA3, not network segments for public servers.","Malware is malicious software, while a DMZ is a network design used for defense.","Alternate recovery sites (hot, warm or cold sites) are for business continuity, not for hosting public-facing servers."],1],["ic48",0,4,"Which describes Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)?",["The provider manages the operating system and application","The customer rents a finished application","The customer rents virtual machines, storage and networking and manages the OS and apps","The customer only rents rack space for its own hardware"],2,"In IaaS the provider runs the physical infrastructure while the customer manages everything from the OS up.","Outline Domain 4",["When the provider manages the OS and application, that describes PaaS or SaaS rather than IaaS.","Renting a finished application is SaaS, where the customer only uses the software.",null,"Renting rack space for your own hardware is colocation, which is not a cloud service model."],2],["ic49",0,4,"Which cloud characteristic lets resources grow and shrink automatically with demand?",["Measured service","Broad network access","Rapid elasticity","Resource pooling"],2,"Rapid elasticity is the ability to scale resources quickly up or down.","Outline Domain 4",["Measured service means usage is metered and billed; it does not describe automatic scaling.","Broad network access means services are reachable over the network from many device types, not that they scale with demand.",null,"Resource pooling means the provider shares resources among many tenants; it enables elasticity but is not the scaling ability itself."],1],["ic50",0,4,"What does defense in depth mean?",["Using multiple layers of controls so one failure does not expose assets","Relying on one strong firewall","Using only encryption","Relying only on user training"],0,"Layered controls mean an attacker must defeat several defenses, not just one.","Outline Domain 4",[null,"Relying on one firewall is a single point of failure, the opposite of layered defense.","Encryption alone is just one control; if keys are stolen or access controls fail, nothing else protects the assets.","User training alone is a single layer, and people will still make mistakes without technical controls behind them."],1],["ic51",0,5,"Which incident response phase comes first?",["Containment","Eradication","Recovery","Preparation"],3,"Preparation (plans, team, tools, training) happens before any incident is detected.","Outline Domain 5",["Containment happens only after an incident has been detected and analyzed, so it cannot be first.","Eradication removes the threat after containment, well after the lifecycle begins.","Recovery restores normal operations near the end of the lifecycle, after eradication.",null],1],["ic52",0,5,"Which algorithm would you use to check that a downloaded file was not altered?",["AES","SHA-256","RSA","TLS"],1,"SHA-256 is a hash function; matching hashes show the file is unchanged.","Outline Domain 5",["AES is a symmetric encryption algorithm for confidentiality; it does not produce a checksum to verify file integrity.",null,"RSA is an asymmetric algorithm for encryption and signatures, not a hash for comparing file contents.","TLS is a protocol that secures data in transit; it is not an algorithm for checking a downloaded file after the fact."],1],["ic53",0,5,"Why do organizations send logs to a central SIEM?",["To make laptops faster","To correlate events across systems and alert on suspicious patterns","To replace backups","To encrypt hard drives"],1,"A SIEM aggregates and correlates logs, making attacks spanning several systems visible.","Outline Domain 5",["Sending logs to a SIEM does not speed up laptops; log forwarding actually uses a little endpoint resource.",null,"A SIEM stores and analyzes logs; it cannot restore systems or data the way backups do.","Disk encryption is an endpoint control and is not a function of a SIEM."],2],["ic54",0,5,"Which activity is part of system hardening?",["Installing every optional service","Keeping default passwords","Disabling logging","Removing unneeded services, changing defaults and applying patches"],3,"Hardening reduces the attack surface by removing what is not needed and securing what remains.","Outline Domain 5",["Installing every optional service increases the attack surface, the opposite of hardening.","Default passwords are widely published, so keeping them makes systems easier to compromise.","Disabling logging removes visibility into attacks and is not part of hardening.",null],2],["ic55",0,5,"What does a recovery point objective (RPO) define?",["The maximum acceptable data loss, measured in time","The maximum acceptable downtime","The total cost of recovery","The number of backup copies kept"],0,"RPO sets how much data, in time, can be lost; RTO sets how long systems can be down.","Outline Domain 5",[null,"Maximum acceptable downtime is described by the recovery time objective (RTO), not the RPO.","Recovery cost is a budget consideration, not what the RPO measures.","The number of backup copies is a backup policy detail; RPO defines how much data loss, in time, is acceptable."],1],["ic56",0,5,"A team has contained ransomware, removed it and restored systems. What should it do next?",["Detection","Preparation","Hold a lessons-learned review","Identification"],2,"Post-incident activity captures what went well and what to improve, closing the lifecycle.","Outline Domain 5",["Detection happens at the start of handling an incident, not after systems have already been restored.","Preparation is the initial phase before incidents occur; the team should first capture lessons that will feed back into it.",null,"Identification of the incident already happened before containment, so it is not the next step after recovery."],2],["ic57",0,5,"What does a data classification label mainly determine?",["Which hardware vendor to buy","The file size limit","How the data must be handled, such as encryption and who may access it","Which backup brand to use"],2,"Classification drives handling rules for protection, access, retention and destruction.","Outline Domain 5",["Classification labels describe data sensitivity, not which hardware vendor to purchase.","Classification does not set file size limits; it sets how the data must be protected and handled.",null,"Choosing a backup brand is a procurement decision; classification may influence backup requirements but not the brand."],1],["ic58",0,5,"A company is disposing of laptops with SSDs that held sensitive data. What is the best method?",["Physical destruction or cryptographic erasure","Deleting the files","A quick format","Renaming the folders"],0,"Deleting or formatting leaves recoverable data; destruction or crypto-erase makes it unrecoverable.","Outline Domain 5",[null,"Deleting files only removes references to the data, which can often still be recovered with forensic tools.","A quick format rewrites file system structures but leaves most of the underlying data recoverable.","Renaming folders changes only labels and leaves all of the sensitive data intact."],3],["ic59",0,5,"Alice wants to send Bob a confidential message using asymmetric encryption. Which key does she encrypt with?",["Alice's private key","Alice's public key","Bob's private key","Bob's public key"],3,"Encrypting with Bob's public key means only Bob's private key can decrypt it.","Outline Domain 5",["Encrypting with Alice's private key lets anyone with her public key decrypt it, which provides origin proof but not confidentiality.","Anything encrypted with Alice's public key can only be decrypted with Alice's private key, so Bob could not read it.","Alice should never have Bob's private key; only Bob holds it, and it is used to decrypt, not encrypt, the message.",null],3],["ic60",0,5,"What is the purpose of a business continuity plan?",["To restore only IT systems","To keep critical business functions running during and after a disruption","To discipline staff after incidents","To replace the incident response plan"],1,"BCP keeps essential operations going; disaster recovery focuses on restoring IT.","Outline Domain 5",["Restoring IT systems is the focus of disaster recovery, which is only one part of keeping the whole business running.",null,"Disciplining staff is an HR matter and is not the purpose of continuity planning.","A BCP complements the incident response plan rather than replacing it; each covers a different scope."],1],["x1",0,1,"A company posts signs at its entrance warning that trespassers will be prosecuted. Which type of security control are these signs acting as?",["Preventive","Detective","Deterrent","Corrective"],2,"Warning signs discourage people from attempting an action without physically stopping them, which is the definition of a deterrent control.","Domain 1: Security control types",["Preventive controls physically stop an action before it happens; a sign only discourages.","Detective controls identify incidents after they occur, not warn people in advance.",null,"Corrective controls fix damage after an incident, which a warning sign does not do."],2],["x2",0,1,"After a malware infection, an administrator wipes the affected server and restores it from a clean backup. Which control category does this restoration represent?",["Deterrent","Preventive","Detective","Corrective"],3,"Restoring a system to a good state after an incident fixes the damage, which is a corrective control.","Domain 1: Security control types",["Deterrent controls discourage behavior; they do not repair a compromised system.","Preventive controls stop an incident before it happens, not restore afterward.","Detective controls only identify that something occurred; they do not fix it.",null],2],["x3",0,1,"A risk analyst calculates the annualized loss expectancy in dollars to justify the cost of a control. Which type of risk assessment is being performed?",["Qualitative","Quantitative","Inherent","Residual"],1,"Assigning monetary values such as ALE is a quantitative assessment; qualitative methods use ratings like high or low instead.","Domain 1: Risk assessment",["Qualitative assessment uses ratings like high or low, not dollar figures such as ALE.",null,"Inherent risk is the risk level before controls, not a method of assessment.","Residual risk is what remains after controls, not an assessment technique."],3],["x4",0,1,"A privacy officer wants individuals to control how their personal data is collected and used. Which concept does this goal most directly support?",["Availability","Non-repudiation","Privacy","Integrity"],2,"Giving people control over how their personal information is handled is the core idea of privacy.","Domain 1: Privacy",["Availability concerns keeping systems accessible, not personal data control.","Non-repudiation prevents denying an action; it is not about personal data control.",null,"Integrity concerns preventing unauthorized changes, not who controls personal data."],2],["x5",0,1,"Leadership reviews a low-likelihood, low-impact risk, documents the decision, and chooses to take no further action. Which risk treatment is this?",["Mitigation","Acceptance","Avoidance","Transference"],1,"Knowingly documenting a risk and deciding to live with it, rather than acting, is risk acceptance.","Domain 1: Risk treatment",["Mitigation means adding controls to lower the risk, which was not done here.",null,"Avoidance means stopping the risky activity entirely, not keeping it as is.","Transference shifts the risk to a third party, such as insurance, which did not happen."],3],["x6",0,1,"An application allows sign-in only when the request originates from an approved location and denies all others. Which authentication factor is being used?",["Something you know","Something you have","Something you are","Somewhere you are"],3,"Using the user's location to allow or deny access is the 'somewhere you are' (location) factor.","Domain 1: Authentication factors",["Something you know is a password or PIN, not the request's location.","Something you have is a token or device, not a location.","Something you are is a biometric trait, not a location.",null],3],["x7",0,1,"A file server uses redundant disks and a backup power supply so it keeps serving files if a disk or the power fails. Which security principle does this support?",["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability","Non-repudiation"],2,"Redundancy keeps a service reachable despite component failure, which protects availability.","Domain 1: CIA triad",["Confidentiality concerns keeping data secret, not keeping the service running.","Integrity concerns preventing unauthorized changes, not uptime.",null,"Non-repudiation concerns proving who acted, not keeping a service available."],2],["x8",0,1,"According to guidance on the ISC2 Code of Ethics, if two canons appear to conflict, how should a member resolve the conflict?",["Follow whichever is easiest","Ignore both canons","Ask the client to decide","Apply the canons in the order they are listed"],3,"The canons are meant to be applied in their stated order of priority when they seem to conflict.","Domain 1: ISC2 Code of Ethics",["Choosing the easiest option ignores the canons' defined priority order.","Ignoring the canons contradicts a member's obligation to uphold them.","The canons, not the client, determine how conflicts are resolved.",null],3],["x9",0,1,"An employee inserts a smart card and enters a PIN to log in. The smart card itself represents which authentication factor?",["Something you know","Something you are","Somewhere you are","Something you have"],3,"A physical smart card is a token you possess, so it is a 'something you have' factor; the PIN is 'something you know'.","Domain 1: Authentication factors",["Something you know refers to the PIN, not the physical card.","Something you are is a biometric trait, not a possessed object.","Somewhere you are is location, which the card does not represent.",null],1],["x10",0,1,"A security team documents 'organized crime groups seeking financial gain' as a source of potential harm. What are they identifying?",["A vulnerability","A control","A threat","An asset"],2,"An actor or event that could cause harm is a threat; a vulnerability is the weakness it would exploit.","Domain 1: Risk terminology",["A vulnerability is a weakness, not the actor that would exploit it.","A control is a safeguard, not a source of harm.",null,"An asset is something of value to protect, not a source of harm."],1],["x11",0,1,"A door lock stops an unauthorized person from entering a wiring closet. Which control function does the lock primarily perform?",["Detective","Corrective","Preventive","Compensating"],2,"A lock stops the unwanted action before it happens, which is a preventive control function.","Domain 1: Security control types",["Detective controls identify an event after it happens, not block entry.","Corrective controls repair damage afterward, not prevent entry.",null,"A compensating control substitutes for another; the lock directly prevents access."],2],["x12",0,1,"An administrator wants to confirm that a configuration file has not been altered since it was approved. Which technique best verifies this?",["Comparing a cryptographic hash of the file","Encrypting the file with a public key","Compressing the file","Renaming the file"],0,"A cryptographic hash changes if any content changes, so matching hashes prove the file is unaltered.","Domain 1: Integrity",[null,"Public-key encryption protects confidentiality, not detection of alteration.","Compression only reduces size and does not reveal tampering.","Renaming a file does nothing to verify its contents are unchanged."],2],["x13",0,1,"After a company applies controls to reduce a risk, some risk still remains. What is this remaining risk called?",["Inherent risk","Total risk","Transferred risk","Residual risk"],3,"The risk left over after controls are applied is residual risk; inherent risk is the level before controls.","Domain 1: Risk terminology",["Inherent risk is the level before any controls are applied, not what remains.","Total risk is the full exposure, not the leftover after controls.","Transferred risk is passed to another party, not what stays with the organization.",null],1],["x14",0,1,"Before assessing risk, a team catalogs the servers, data, and applications the business depends on. What are these items called?",["Threats","Vulnerabilities","Assets","Controls"],2,"Things of value that the organization needs to protect are its assets.","Domain 1: Risk terminology",["Threats are sources of harm, not the valuable items being catalogued.","Vulnerabilities are weaknesses, not the resources the business relies on.",null,"Controls are safeguards, not the resources being protected."],2],["x15",0,1,"Regulators ask a company to explain how its AI model reached a lending decision. Which quality of trustworthy AI does this request emphasize?",["Elasticity","Redundancy","Throughput","Transparency"],3,"Being able to explain how an AI reaches a decision is transparency, a key element of trustworthy AI.","Domain 1: AI and CIA",["Elasticity is a cloud scaling property, unrelated to explaining AI decisions.","Redundancy supports availability, not explainability of AI decisions.","Throughput measures processing volume, not the ability to explain decisions.",null],2],["x16",0,2,"A document sets the minimum security configuration that every company laptop must meet. Which type of governance document is this?",["Guideline","Policy","Baseline","Procedure"],2,"A baseline defines the minimum acceptable configuration or security level; a policy states high-level intent.","Domain 2: Policies and standards",["A guideline is only a recommendation, not a mandatory minimum configuration.","A policy states high-level intent, not a specific minimum configuration.",null,"A procedure gives step-by-step instructions, not a minimum configuration standard."],1],["x17",0,2,"A retailer that stores and processes customer credit card data must comply with which requirement set?",["PCI DSS","HIPAA","GDPR","SOX"],0,"PCI DSS governs the handling of payment card data; HIPAA covers health data and GDPR covers EU personal data.","Domain 2: Laws and regulations",[null,"HIPAA covers protected health information, not payment card data.","GDPR covers EU personal data, not specifically payment cards.","SOX addresses financial reporting controls, not cardholder data."],1],["x18",0,2,"An organization defines how much it will allow a specific project to deviate from its stated risk appetite. This acceptable deviation is its:",["Risk tolerance","Risk transfer","Risk register","Risk avoidance"],0,"Risk tolerance is the acceptable variation around the broad risk appetite for a given activity.","Domain 2: Risk appetite and tolerance",[null,"Risk transfer shifts risk to a third party; it is not the acceptable deviation.","A risk register is a list of risks, not a measure of acceptable deviation.","Risk avoidance removes the activity; it does not describe acceptable variation."],3],["x19",0,2,"A company is concerned that a software supplier could introduce vulnerabilities into delivered code. This is an example of:",["Insider threat","Third-party (supply chain) risk","Physical risk","Residual risk"],1,"Risk arising from vendors and suppliers is third-party or supply chain risk; an insider threat comes from within.","Domain 2: Third-party risk",["An insider threat comes from within the organization, not from a supplier.",null,"Physical risk relates to facilities and hardware, not supplier-introduced code.","Residual risk is what remains after controls, not a supplier-origin risk."],2],["x20",0,2,"A document recommends, but does not require, that staff use a password manager. What kind of governance document is it?",["Policy","Standard","Procedure","Guideline"],3,"Optional recommendations are guidelines; policies and standards are mandatory.","Domain 2: Policies and standards",["A policy is mandatory, but this document only recommends.","A standard sets mandatory requirements, unlike an optional recommendation.","A procedure gives required steps, not optional advice.",null],1],["x21",0,2,"Which law primarily governs the protection of personal data belonging to individuals in the European Union?",["PCI DSS","HIPAA","GLBA","GDPR"],3,"The GDPR is the EU regulation for personal data protection; the others cover payment cards, health, and US financial data.","Domain 2: Laws and regulations",["PCI DSS governs payment card data, not general EU personal data.","HIPAA covers US health information, not EU personal data broadly.","GLBA covers US financial institutions, not EU personal data.",null],1],["x22",0,2,"Which group in an organization is responsible for setting the risk appetite?",["The help desk","External attackers","End users","Senior leadership"],3,"Setting risk appetite is a governance decision owned by senior leadership and the board.","Domain 2: Governance and leadership",["The help desk handles support tasks, not setting organizational risk appetite.","External attackers are a threat, not decision-makers for the organization.","End users follow policy; they do not set risk appetite.",null],2],["x23",0,2,"Which metric is designed to indicate performance against a goal rather than warn of rising risk exposure?",["Key risk indicator","Recovery time objective","Annualized loss expectancy","Key performance indicator"],3,"A KPI measures progress toward a goal; a KRI is chosen specifically to signal increasing risk.","Domain 2: Program metrics",["A key risk indicator warns of rising risk, not performance toward a goal.","A recovery time objective is a continuity target, not a performance metric here.","Annualized loss expectancy is a risk cost estimate, not a performance measure.",null],2],["x24",0,2,"Which of the following is the best evidence that a security awareness program is changing employee behavior?",["Number of training slides","Total employees hired","Reduced click rate on phishing simulations","Number of policies written"],2,"A falling phishing-simulation click rate shows behavior is changing; counting slides or policies does not.","Domain 2: Security awareness",["Counting slides measures effort, not any change in employee behavior.","The number of employees hired says nothing about awareness behavior.",null,"The number of policies written does not show behavior has changed."],3],["x25",0,2,"An auditor finds staff are skipping a required approval step. Which document should define that mandatory requirement at a high level?",["Guideline","Policy","Newsletter","Marketing brochure"],1,"Mandatory requirements are stated in a policy; guidelines are only recommendations and the others are not governance documents.","Domain 2: Policies and standards",["A guideline is only a recommendation, not a mandatory required step.",null,"A newsletter is informal communication, not a governance document.","A marketing brochure is promotional material, not a governance document."],2],["x26",0,3,"A fully cleared analyst is denied access to a classified project that is unrelated to their duties. Which principle enforces this restriction?",["Separation of duties","Need to know","Single sign-on","Federation"],1,"Need to know limits access to information a person actually requires for their job, even if they have clearance.","Domain 3: Least privilege and need to know",["Separation of duties splits a task among people; it is not about topical access limits.",null,"Single sign-on is an authentication convenience, not an access-limiting principle.","Federation extends identity across organizations; it does not restrict by need."],3],["x27",0,3,"A user signs into a partner company's website using their own employer's identity provider. What makes this possible?",["Identity federation","Mandatory access control","Privilege creep","Tailgating"],0,"Federation lets one organization trust another's identity provider so users can log in across domains.","Domain 3: Federation and SSO",[null,"Mandatory access control enforces labels; it does not enable cross-company login.","Privilege creep is unwanted access buildup, not a login mechanism.","Tailgating is a physical entry attack, unrelated to federated login."],2],["x28",0,3,"On a new hire's first day, accounts and access are created for them based on their assigned role. Which identity lifecycle stage is this?",["Deprovisioning","Access review","Recertification","Provisioning"],3,"Creating identities and granting initial access is provisioning; deprovisioning removes access when someone leaves.","Domain 3: Identity lifecycle",["Deprovisioning removes access when a user leaves, the opposite of onboarding.","An access review checks existing access; it does not create it on day one.","Recertification confirms existing access remains valid, not initial creation.",null],2],["x29",0,3,"A firewall permits traffic only during business hours, regardless of who the user is. Which access control type does this represent?",["Discretionary","Role-based","Rule-based","Mandatory"],2,"Rule-based control applies conditions such as time of day to everyone; RBAC would decide by the user's role instead.","Domain 3: Access control models",["Discretionary control lets owners decide access, not a fixed time rule for all.","Role-based control decides by the user's role, not a blanket time condition.",null,"Mandatory control uses labels and clearances, not time-of-day rules."],3],["x30",0,3,"Which type of solution vaults, rotates, and monitors the credentials used for administrative accounts?",["Privileged access management","Single sign-on","A guest VLAN","A honeypot"],0,"PAM specifically secures, rotates, and audits privileged credentials; SSO handles ordinary user logins.","Domain 3: Privileged access management",[null,"Single sign-on simplifies user logins; it does not vault admin credentials.","A guest VLAN isolates visitor traffic, unrelated to privileged credentials.","A honeypot is a decoy system, not a credential management tool."],2],["x31",0,3,"An attacker carrying boxes asks an employee to hold the secure door open for them. Which threat is this?",["Phishing","Tailgating","Spoofing","DDoS"],1,"Following an authorized person through a controlled door is tailgating; phishing and spoofing are not physical entry.","Domain 3: Physical access controls",["Phishing uses deceptive messages, not physical door access.",null,"Spoofing falsifies identity in data, not physical entry through a door.","A DDoS floods a service; it is not a physical entry technique."],2],["x32",0,3,"Periodically moving employees through different sensitive positions helps expose hidden fraud. What is this practice called?",["Job rotation","Least privilege","Federation","Provisioning"],0,"Job rotation moves people through duties so wrongdoing that depends on one person staying put is uncovered.","Domain 3: Separation of duties",[null,"Least privilege limits access levels; it is not the practice of rotating staff.","Federation links identity across organizations, unrelated to rotating duties.","Provisioning creates accounts and access, not rotation of personnel."],1],["x33",0,3,"A badge system records every entry to the data center along with the time and identity. What does this recording primarily support?",["Prevention of entry","Accounting and auditing of access","Encryption of badges","Deprovisioning"],1,"Keeping a record of who accessed what and when is the accounting or auditing function.","Domain 3: Accounting and access reviews",["A record of entries documents access; it does not by itself prevent entry.",null,"Encrypting badges protects credentials, not the logging of who entered.","Deprovisioning removes access; it is not the recording of entries."],3],["x34",0,3,"In a mandatory access control (MAC) system, why can a user not simply share a file they own with a colleague?",["Access is enforced by system-wide labels and clearances, not user choice","Files cannot be shared in any operating system","The colleague does not have a username","MAC applies only to printers"],0,"In MAC the system enforces access using labels and clearances set by policy, so owners cannot grant access on their own.","Domain 3: Access control models",[null,"Files can be shared in many systems; the constraint is specific to MAC.","Lacking a username is not why MAC blocks owner-granted sharing.","MAC governs data and systems broadly, not only printers."],3],["x35",0,3,"Every quarter, managers must confirm that each team member still needs their current access. What is this process called?",["Access recertification","Single sign-on","Provisioning","Password rotation"],0,"Periodically confirming that access is still appropriate is access recertification or review.","Domain 3: Periodic access reviews",[null,"Single sign-on is an authentication feature, not a periodic access check.","Provisioning grants initial access; it does not confirm ongoing need.","Password rotation changes credentials, not whether access is still needed."],2],["x36",0,3,"An employee moves from finance to marketing but keeps their old finance system access. What should deprovisioning on transfer prevent?",["Tailgating","DDoS","Data classification","Privilege creep"],3,"Accumulating unneeded access across role changes is privilege creep, which timely removal on transfer prevents.","Domain 3: Identity lifecycle",["Tailgating is a physical entry attack, not an access-accumulation issue.","A DDoS is an availability attack, unrelated to leftover access on transfer.","Data classification labels information; it does not concern access buildup.",null],2],["x37",0,3,"To reduce insider fraud, the person who requests new access must not be the same person who approves it. Which principle is applied?",["Least privilege","Separation of duties","Federation","Elasticity"],1,"Splitting request and approval between two people is separation of duties; least privilege limits how much access is granted.","Domain 3: Separation of duties",["Least privilege limits how much access is granted, not who approves it.",null,"Federation links identity across organizations, not approval separation.","Elasticity is a cloud scaling property, unrelated to approval duties."],3],["x38",0,4,"A helpdesk technician connects to a Windows desktop remotely using Remote Desktop Protocol. Which default port does that use?",["22","443","3389","53"],2,"RDP uses TCP 3389 by default; 22 is SSH, 443 is HTTPS, and 53 is DNS.","Domain 4: Common ports",["Port 22 is used by SSH, not Remote Desktop Protocol.","Port 443 is used by HTTPS, not RDP.",null,"Port 53 is used by DNS, not RDP."],1],["x39",0,4,"Which port does a standard DNS name lookup normally use?",["80","53","25","389"],1,"DNS name resolution uses port 53; port 80 is HTTP, port 25 is SMTP for email, and port 389 is LDAP directory access.","Domain 4: Common ports",["Port 80 is used by HTTP, not DNS lookups.",null,"Port 25 is used by SMTP, not DNS.","Port 389 is used by LDAP, not DNS."],1],["x40",0,4,"Email transferred between mail servers commonly uses which port?",["110","143","25","3389"],2,"SMTP uses port 25 for server-to-server mail; 110 is POP3 and 143 is IMAP for retrieval.","Domain 4: Common ports",["Port 110 is used by POP3 for mail retrieval, not server-to-server sending.","Port 143 is used by IMAP for mail retrieval, not sending between servers.",null,"Port 3389 is used by RDP, not email transfer."],1],["x41",0,4,"A company keeps sensitive records in its private cloud but bursts to a public cloud during peak demand. Which deployment model is this?",["Public","Community","Hybrid","Private"],2,"Combining private and public clouds that work together is a hybrid cloud deployment.","Domain 4: Cloud deployment models",["A public cloud alone does not include the company's private cloud portion.","A community cloud is shared by similar organizations, not a private-plus-public mix.",null,"A private cloud alone excludes the public-cloud bursting described."],3],["x42",0,4,"A developer deploys code to a service where the provider manages the operating system and runtime but the developer manages the application. Which service model is this?",["IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","On-premises"],1,"PaaS provides a managed platform for applications while the provider handles the OS and runtime beneath it.","Domain 4: Cloud service models",["In IaaS the customer manages the operating system, unlike the described service.",null,"In SaaS the provider manages the whole application, not just the platform.","On-premises means the customer runs all infrastructure themselves."],2],["x43",0,4,"A data center applies security policies between individual workloads, not just at the network edge. What is this approach called?",["Micro-segmentation","Port forwarding","Network address translation","Broadcasting"],0,"Enforcing controls between individual workloads is micro-segmentation, a finer-grained form of segmentation.","Domain 4: Network design",[null,"Port forwarding redirects specific traffic, not policy between workloads.","Network address translation remaps addresses; it does not segment workloads.","Broadcasting sends traffic to all hosts, the opposite of isolating workloads."],3],["x44",0,4,"An attacker secretly relays and can alter the messages between two parties who believe they are communicating directly. Which attack is this?",["Denial of service","Data poisoning","Tailgating","Man-in-the-middle"],3,"Intercepting and possibly altering traffic between two parties is a man-in-the-middle attack.","Domain 4: Network threats",["A denial of service disrupts availability; it does not relay messages secretly.","Data poisoning corrupts training data, not live communications between parties.","Tailgating is a physical entry attack, not message interception.",null],2],["x45",0,4,"Which device filters traffic between network segments based on rules about addresses, ports, and protocols?",["Switch","Firewall","Hub","Wireless access point"],1,"A firewall enforces rules to allow or block traffic between segments; a switch or hub just forwards frames.","Domain 4: Network defenses",["A switch forwards frames within a segment; it does not filter by rules.",null,"A hub simply repeats traffic to all ports without filtering.","A wireless access point provides connectivity, not rule-based filtering."],1],["x46",0,4,"A remote worker needs an encrypted tunnel back to the corporate network over the public internet. Which technology provides this?",["VLAN","DMZ","VPN","NAT"],2,"A VPN creates an encrypted tunnel across untrusted networks; a VLAN and DMZ are network segments, not tunnels.","Domain 4: Network defenses",["A VLAN segments a local network; it does not tunnel across the internet.","A DMZ is a screened subnet for public services, not an encrypted tunnel.",null,"NAT translates addresses; it does not create an encrypted tunnel."],2],["x47",0,4,"A cloud customer is billed based on the exact amount of storage and compute they consume. Which cloud characteristic is this?",["Resource pooling","Measured service","Broad network access","Multitenancy"],1,"Metering and billing by actual usage is the measured service characteristic of cloud computing.","Domain 4: Cloud characteristics",["Resource pooling shares infrastructure among tenants, not usage-based billing.",null,"Broad network access means reaching services over the network, not metering.","Multitenancy means many customers share resources, not billing by usage."],3],["x48",0,4,"In an Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) deployment, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system?",["The cloud provider","No one","The end user's ISP","The customer"],3,"Under IaaS the provider runs the physical infrastructure while the customer manages the OS upward, including patches.","Domain 4: Shared responsibility model",["In IaaS the provider manages the physical infrastructure and hypervisor, not the guest OS.","Guest OS patching is a defined responsibility, so it is not left to no one.","The ISP provides connectivity and does not patch a customer's guest OS.",null],3],["x49",0,4,"Packets arrive on the network claiming a source IP address that does not really belong to the sender in order to bypass a filter. Which technique is this?",["Spoofing","Segmentation","Encryption","Load balancing"],0,"Forging a source address to appear as a trusted host is spoofing.","Domain 4: Network threats",[null,"Segmentation divides a network; it is not the act of forging source addresses.","Encryption protects data confidentiality, not forging source addresses.","Load balancing distributes traffic; it does not falsify source addresses."],2],["x50",0,4,"At which layer of the TCP/IP model do TCP and UDP operate, using port numbers to identify applications?",["Application","Transport","Internet","Link"],1,"TCP and UDP with their port numbers operate at the transport layer of the TCP/IP model.","Domain 4: TCP/IP model",["The application layer handles services like HTTP, not TCP/UDP ports directly.",null,"The internet layer handles IP addressing and routing, not port numbers.","The link layer handles local frame delivery, not transport ports."],1],["x51",0,5,"Analysts isolate an infected laptop from the network to stop malware from spreading to other systems. Which incident response phase is this?",["Detection","Containment","Recovery","Preparation"],1,"Limiting the spread of an active incident is containment; recovery comes later when systems are restored.","Domain 5: Incident response lifecycle",["Detection is recognizing the incident, which already happened before isolating.",null,"Recovery restores systems to normal after the threat is removed, not now.","Preparation happens before any incident, not during an active response."],2],["x52",0,5,"Two systems share the same secret key to encrypt and decrypt large amounts of data quickly. Which type of encryption is this?",["Asymmetric","Hashing","Symmetric","Steganography"],2,"Using one shared secret key for both encryption and decryption is symmetric encryption; asymmetric uses a key pair.","Domain 5: Encryption",["Asymmetric encryption uses a public and private key pair, not one shared key.","Hashing is one-way and does not decrypt data.",null,"Steganography hides data in other data; it is not shared-key encryption."],2],["x53",0,5,"A business decides its order system must be back in operation within four hours of an outage. Which metric expresses this target?",["Recovery point objective","Mean time between failures","Annualized rate of occurrence","Recovery time objective"],3,"The maximum acceptable downtime before restoration is the recovery time objective; RPO measures acceptable data loss.","Domain 5: Business continuity",["A recovery point objective measures acceptable data loss, not downtime.","Mean time between failures measures reliability, not restoration targets.","Annualized rate of occurrence estimates event frequency, not downtime.",null],2],["x54",0,5,"Before writing a continuity plan, a company identifies which functions are most critical and the impact of losing them. What is this analysis called?",["Business impact analysis","Penetration test","Access review","Patch cycle"],0,"A business impact analysis identifies critical functions and the effect of their loss, feeding RTO and RPO decisions.","Domain 5: Business continuity",[null,"A penetration test probes for vulnerabilities, not criticality of functions.","An access review checks user permissions, not business function impact.","A patch cycle schedules updates; it does not analyze business impact."],3],["x55",0,5,"A proposed firewall rule change must be reviewed and approved before it is applied to production systems. Which process governs this?",["Change management","Incident response","Data classification","Federation"],0,"Reviewing and approving changes before they reach production is change management, which reduces outages and errors.","Domain 5: Change management",[null,"Incident response handles active incidents, not planned production changes.","Data classification labels information; it does not approve changes.","Federation links identity across organizations, unrelated to change approval."],3],["x56",0,5,"A policy states that financial records must be kept for seven years and then securely destroyed. Which data security concept is this?",["Data retention","Encryption","Segmentation","Non-repudiation"],0,"Rules on how long to keep data before disposal are data retention; encryption protects it but does not set a timeline.","Domain 5: Data security",[null,"Encryption protects data confidentiality but does not set a keep-and-destroy timeline.","Segmentation isolates networks; it does not govern how long data is kept.","Non-repudiation proves who acted; it does not concern retention periods."],2],["x57",0,5,"After containing a breach, the team removes the attacker's malware and closes the vulnerability that was exploited. Which phase is this?",["Detection","Eradication","Preparation","Lessons learned"],1,"Removing the threat and its root cause from the environment is eradication, which follows containment.","Domain 5: Incident response lifecycle",["Detection recognizes the incident; the malware has already been found here.",null,"Preparation happens before incidents, not while removing an active threat.","Lessons learned is the final review phase, after eradication and recovery."],2],["x58",0,5,"An organization keeps a fully equipped, running duplicate facility ready to take over operations almost immediately. Which recovery option is this?",["Cold site","Warm site","Hot site","Reciprocal agreement"],2,"A hot site is fully equipped and running so operations can switch over quickly; a cold site has little or no equipment ready.","Domain 5: Disaster recovery",["A cold site has little equipment ready and cannot take over immediately.","A warm site is partially equipped and needs setup before taking over.",null,"A reciprocal agreement shares another organization's site, not a ready duplicate."],3],["x59",0,5,"A team regularly scans systems, prioritizes findings by risk, and tracks their remediation over time. What is this ongoing process called?",["Vulnerability management","Incident eradication","Data labeling","Change freeze"],0,"The continuous cycle of finding, prioritizing, and fixing weaknesses is vulnerability management.","Domain 5: Vulnerability management",[null,"Incident eradication removes a specific threat; it is not the ongoing scan cycle.","Data labeling classifies information; it does not find and fix weaknesses.","A change freeze halts changes; it is not vulnerability remediation."],2],["x60",0,5,"A SOC analyst reviews incoming SIEM alerts to decide which need investigation and which are false positives. What is this activity called?",["Event triage","Backup rotation","Key rotation","Provisioning"],0,"Sorting and prioritizing alerts to decide what to investigate is event triage.","Domain 5: Logging and monitoring",[null,"Backup rotation manages backup media, not alert investigation.","Key rotation changes cryptographic keys, unrelated to reviewing alerts.","Provisioning creates accounts and access, not alert prioritization."],3],["x61",0,1,"Ransomware encrypts a company's file shares so staff cannot open any documents. Logs show no data left the network. Which security principle was most directly affected?",["Confidentiality","Non-repudiation","Availability","Authentication"],2,"The files still exist but cannot be used, which is a loss of availability. Confidentiality is tempting because ransomware is often linked to data theft, but here nothing was disclosed.","Domain 1: CIA triad",["Confidentiality is about unauthorized disclosure, and the logs show no data was taken.","Non-repudiation proves who performed an action; it is not what the encryption disrupted.",null,"Authentication verifies identities; the attack did not change how users prove who they are."],1],["x62",0,1,"A laptop holding an unencrypted spreadsheet of customer names and account numbers is stolen from a car. Which security principle is most at risk?",["Confidentiality","Availability","Integrity","Accountability"],0,"Unencrypted sensitive data in the wrong hands is a loss of confidentiality. Availability is tempting because the laptop is gone, but the greater harm is that unauthorized people can read the data.","Domain 1: CIA triad",[null,"Other copies of the data may still be available; the main concern is who can now read it.","Nothing indicates the data was altered; the danger is exposure to the thief.","Accountability tracks user actions on systems, which is not the core issue in a theft of data."],1],["x63",0,1,"An attacker on the network path changes the amount in an online bank transfer from 100 to 10,000 before it reaches the bank. Which security principle has been violated?",["Confidentiality","Availability","Privacy","Integrity"],3,"Integrity means data is not altered without authorization, and changing the transfer amount violates it. Confidentiality is tempting because the attacker saw the message, but the damage comes from the modification.","Domain 1: CIA triad",["The problem is not that someone read the transfer but that its content was changed.","The transfer still went through, so the service was not made unavailable.","Privacy concerns the handling of personal data, not unauthorized changes to a transaction.",null],1],["x64",0,1,"A website asks users for a password and then for the answer to a security question such as their first pet's name. Why does this NOT count as multi-factor authentication?",["Security questions are never stored securely","Both are something the user knows","The password must be entered second","Only biometric factors count toward multi-factor authentication"],1,"MFA requires factors from different categories, such as something you know plus something you have or are, and here both are knowledge. The claim that only biometrics count is tempting, but possession factors like tokens also qualify.","Domain 1: Authentication factors",["Storage practices do not decide whether factors are different types; both are still knowledge.",null,"The order in which factors are entered has no effect on whether they count as multiple factors.","Something you have, like a token, also counts; biometrics are only one of the factor types."],2],["x65",0,1,"A server is valued at $200,000. Analysts estimate that a flood would destroy 25% of its value. What is the single loss expectancy (SLE) for a flood?",["$25,000","$50,000","$200,000","$800,000"],1,"SLE equals asset value times exposure factor: $200,000 x 0.25 = $50,000. Using the full asset value is tempting, but a flood is only expected to destroy a quarter of it.","Domain 1: Quantitative risk assessment",["This would be a 12.5% exposure factor, not the 25% the analysts estimated.",null,"This is the full asset value, which ignores the 25% exposure factor.","This multiplies the asset value by 4 instead of by 25%."],2],["x66",0,1,"A risk has a single loss expectancy of $50,000 and is expected to occur once every five years. A control that would eliminate the risk costs $15,000 per year. Based on annualized loss expectancy alone, what should management conclude?",["The control is clearly justified because the single loss expectancy of $50,000 exceeds its yearly cost","The expected annual loss is $250,000, so the control is cheap","The control saves exactly $35,000 per year","The control costs more per year than the $10,000 expected annual loss"],3,"ALE = SLE x ARO = $50,000 x 0.2 = $10,000 per year, which is less than the $15,000 annual control cost. Comparing the control cost with the SLE is tempting, but costs must be compared on the same yearly basis.","Domain 1: Quantitative risk assessment (ALE)",["SLE is the loss from one event; comparing it with a yearly cost ignores how rarely the event happens.","This multiplies by 5 instead of by an ARO of 0.2, overstating the yearly loss.","This subtracts the control cost from the SLE, which mixes a one-time loss with a yearly cost.",null],3],["x67",0,1,"After reviewing the risks, a company cancels its plan to store customer payment data on its own servers and never starts the project. Which risk treatment is this?",["Avoidance","Mitigation","Transference","Acceptance"],0,"Stopping the activity that creates the risk is risk avoidance. Mitigation is tempting because it also lowers risk, but it keeps the activity and adds controls.","Domain 1: Risk treatment",[null,"Mitigation would keep the project and add controls to reduce the risk.","Transference would shift the financial impact to another party, such as an insurer.","Acceptance would continue the project and knowingly live with the risk."],1],["x68",0,1,"Which of the following is an example of a technical (logical) security control?",["A mandatory annual security awareness training program for all staff","A locked door on the server room","A firewall rule that blocks inbound remote desktop traffic","A background check for new hires"],2,"Technical controls are implemented in hardware or software, like firewall rules. A locked door is tempting because it also blocks access, but it is a physical control.","Domain 1: Security controls",["Training is an administrative control that works through people and policy.","A locked door is a physical control that protects a space.",null,"Background checks are an administrative control applied during hiring."],1],["x69",0,1,"An ISC2 member's employer asks them to keep quiet about a known safety flaw in a medical device that could harm patients. Which canon of the ISC2 Code of Ethics should guide the member first?",["Provide diligent and competent service to principals","Advance and protect the profession","Act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly and legally toward the employer and every colleague involved","Protect society, the common good, necessary public trust and confidence, and the infrastructure"],3,"The canons are applied in order, and protecting society and the common good comes first when duties conflict. Serving principals is tempting because the employer is asking, but that canon ranks lower.","Domain 1: ISC2 Code of Ethics",["Serving principals matters, but it ranks below protecting society when the two conflict.","Advancing the profession is the lowest-priority canon and does not address public harm first.","Acting honorably applies, but the canons are ordered, and protecting society comes first.",null],2],["x70",0,1,"An ISC2 member accepts a paid engagement to assess an industrial control system, a field in which they have no experience, and does not tell the client. Which canon is this most directly at odds with?",["Protect society, the common good and the infrastructure","Provide diligent and competent service to principals","Advance and protect the profession","It violates no canon because the client agreed to pay"],1,"Taking on work beyond one's competence without disclosure fails the duty of diligent and competent service to principals. Protecting society is tempting because control systems are critical, but the direct breach is toward the client.","Domain 1: ISC2 Code of Ethics",["Poor work could eventually affect society, but the direct failure is toward the client being served.",null,"The profession may suffer indirectly, but the main issue is incompetent service to the client.","Payment does not excuse hiding a lack of competence from the client."],3],["x71",0,1,"A hiring model trained on ten years of past decisions consistently scores applicants from one region lower, although no attacker has touched the system. What is the most likely cause?",["Data poisoning by an outside attacker","Theft of the model by a competitor","Bias in the historical training data","A denial-of-service attack on the model"],2,"A model trained on skewed historical decisions learns and repeats that bias, which undermines fairness and trust. Data poisoning is tempting because it also corrupts training data, but it requires a deliberate attacker.","Domain 1: CIA applied to AI systems",["Poisoning involves someone deliberately injecting bad data, and no attacker was involved here.","Model theft copies the model; it would not change how it scores applicants.",null,"A denial-of-service attack makes the model unavailable rather than skewing its results."],2],["x72",0,1,"A company redesigns its sign-up form to collect only the customer's email address instead of full name, birth date and home address, because the service does not need the rest. Which privacy principle is being applied?",["Data minimization","Data retention","Non-repudiation","Data classification"],0,"Data minimization means collecting only the personal data needed for a stated purpose. Retention is tempting because it also limits data, but it deals with how long data is kept.","Domain 1: Privacy",[null,"Retention decides how long data is kept, not how much is collected in the first place.","Non-repudiation proves who performed an action; it has nothing to do with collecting less data.","Classification labels data by sensitivity but does not limit what is collected."],2],["x73",0,1,"Five administrators share a single account called admin to manage a critical server. Why does this arrangement prevent non-repudiation?",["Actions cannot be tied to one specific person","Shared accounts cannot use encryption","The operating system cannot record any log entries for shared accounts","Shared accounts always have weaker passwords"],0,"Non-repudiation requires that actions be attributed to a unique individual who cannot deny them, which a shared account makes impossible. Saying the server cannot log is tempting, but it can log; the logs just cannot show who was behind the account.","Domain 1: Non-repudiation",[null,"Encryption can still be used with a shared account; the issue is identifying who acted.","The server can still log actions, but the logs only show the shared account name.","Password strength is unrelated to proving which individual performed an action."],2],["x74",0,1,"A tall fence with a locked gate is installed around a company's backup generator to stop people from reaching it. How is this control best classified?",["Technical and detective","Administrative and corrective","Physical and preventive","Physical and detective"],2,"A fence is a physical barrier that stops unauthorized access before it happens, making it physical and preventive. Physical and detective is tempting, but detection would be something like a camera or alarm.","Domain 1: Security controls",["A fence is not implemented in software, and it stops access rather than detecting it.","A fence is not a policy, and it acts before an incident rather than fixing one afterward.",null,"The fence is physical, but it blocks access instead of detecting or recording it."],1],["x75",0,2,"A document states that all company systems must use AES with 256-bit keys for encrypting stored data. Which type of governance document is this?",["Policy","Standard","Guideline","Procedure"],1,"Standards set mandatory, specific requirements such as a particular algorithm and key length. A policy is tempting because it is also mandatory, but policies state broad goals rather than specific technologies.","Domain 2: Policies, standards, procedures",["A policy states high-level intent, such as protecting data, without naming specific algorithms.",null,"A guideline is a recommendation, while this document makes a specific technology mandatory.","A procedure gives step-by-step instructions, not a required technology choice."],2],["x76",0,2,"A US clinic stores and shares patients' medical records and must protect that health information by law. Which regulation most directly applies?",["PCI DSS","GDPR","SOX","HIPAA"],3,"HIPAA is the US law that protects the privacy and security of patient health information. GDPR is tempting because it is a major privacy law, but it applies to EU personal data.","Domain 2: Laws and regulations",["PCI DSS covers payment card data, not medical records.","GDPR protects personal data of people in the EU, not US health records specifically.","SOX governs financial reporting for public companies, not patient health data.",null],1],["x77",0,2,"A merchant asks why it must follow PCI DSS even though no government passed it as a law. What is the best answer?",["It is a federal privacy law passed by Congress that applies to every business in every country worldwide","It is an industry standard the merchant is bound to through its agreements with card brands and banks","It is optional guidance with no consequences for ignoring it","It only applies to companies that have already suffered a breach"],1,"PCI DSS is a contractual requirement: merchants agree to it through their card acceptance agreements, and noncompliance can bring fines or loss of card processing. Calling it optional is tempting because it is not a law, but the contracts make it binding.","Domain 2: Contractual requirements",["PCI DSS is not a law passed by any government, even though it is widely required.",null,"Ignoring it can lead to fines or losing the ability to accept cards, so it is not consequence-free.","It applies to any organization that stores, processes or transmits cardholder data, breach or not."],3],["x78",0,2,"A security analyst discovers a serious risk in the sales department's customer application. Who should normally be assigned as the owner of this risk?",["The business manager accountable for the sales application","The security analyst who discovered and documented the risk during testing","The external auditor","The help desk team"],0,"Risk owners are the business leaders accountable for the asset or process, because they decide how the risk is treated. The analyst is tempting because they know the risk best, but security advises while the business owns the decision.","Domain 2: Ownership of risk",[null,"Finding a risk does not make the analyst accountable for the business decision on it.","Auditors assess and report independently; owning risks would compromise that independence.","The help desk supports users and lacks authority over the application's business risks."],2],["x79",0,2,"A company is negotiating a contract with a vendor that will host its customer data. Which contract clause lets the company verify the vendor's security controls later?",["A non-compete clause","A force majeure clause","An intellectual property clause","A right-to-audit clause"],3,"A right-to-audit clause gives the customer the right to assess the vendor's controls, which supports third-party risk management. Force majeure is tempting because it also appears in vendor contracts, but it only deals with events outside the parties' control.","Domain 2: Third-party and vendor risk",["A non-compete clause limits competing business activity, not security verification.","Force majeure excuses performance during events like natural disasters; it grants no audit rights.","An intellectual property clause settles who owns created work, not how controls are checked.",null],2],["x80",0,2,"A large cloud provider will not allow individual customers to perform on-site audits of its data centers. What should a customer request instead to gain assurance about the provider's controls?",["The provider's public marketing brochure describing its security certifications and data center features","A copy of the provider's employee handbook","An independent third-party attestation report, such as a SOC 2 Type II","Nothing, since cloud providers cannot be assessed"],2,"Independent attestation reports, such as SOC 2 Type II, show that an auditor tested the provider's controls over a period of time. Assuming the provider cannot be assessed is tempting, but third-party reports exist for exactly this situation.","Domain 2: Third-party and vendor risk",["Marketing material is not independently verified and gives no reliable assurance.","An employee handbook describes staff rules, not tested security controls.",null,"Providers can be assessed through independent reports even when on-site audits are not allowed."],3],["x81",0,2,"Staff in the finance team who approve wire transfers are frequently targeted by business email compromise. What is the most effective way to adjust the awareness program for them?",["Send them the same general annual slideshow that every other employee receives, with no changes","Remove them from phishing simulations to avoid stress","Provide role-based training focused on payment fraud and verification steps","Only train the IT department about payment fraud"],2,"Role-based training gives high-risk groups content tailored to the threats they actually face, such as verifying payment change requests. The generic annual slideshow is tempting because it is easy, but it rarely changes behavior for specialized risks.","Domain 2: Security awareness training",["Generic annual training does not address the specific fraud patterns this team faces.","Removing them from simulations takes away practice for the group most at risk.",null,"IT staff do not approve payments, so training only them leaves the targeted team unprepared."],2],["x82",0,2,"Over three quarters, the average time to patch critical vulnerabilities rose from 10 days to 30 days, while the security budget stayed flat. How should this measure be interpreted for leadership?",["As proof that the security program has fully met its patching goals for the year","As a measure of employee awareness training results","As a sign that fewer vulnerabilities exist","As a key risk indicator showing growing exposure that may need action"],3,"A rising time to patch signals that the window of exposure is widening, which is exactly what a KRI is meant to flag early. Reading it as fewer vulnerabilities is tempting, but the metric says nothing about their number.","Domain 2: Measuring the program (KRIs)",["A longer patch time shows performance getting worse, not goals being met.","Patch timelines reflect operational processes, not awareness training.","The measure tracks how long fixes take, not how many vulnerabilities exist.",null],3],["x83",0,2,"A security program has little funding and employees routinely ignore its policies, saying management does not care about them. What would most improve this situation?",["Visible support and sponsorship from senior leadership","Adding more technical controls to endpoints and servers without explaining them to staff","Writing longer and more detailed policies","Hiring an external penetration testing firm"],0,"Governance depends on the tone at the top; when leaders visibly back security, funding and compliance follow. More technical controls are tempting as a quick fix, but they do not address the missing leadership commitment.","Domain 2: Governance and leadership",[null,"Unexplained controls tend to breed workarounds and do not fix the lack of leadership support.","Longer policies do not help if staff already ignore the existing ones.","A penetration test finds technical weaknesses but does not change funding or culture."],2],["x84",0,2,"A department cannot meet a new encryption standard for six months because its legacy system does not support it. What is the best governance response?",["Let the department quietly ignore the standard until the legacy system is eventually replaced next year","Document a formal exception with compensating controls, approval by the risk owner and an expiry date","Rewrite the standard so it no longer applies to anyone","Shut down the legacy system immediately regardless of business impact"],1,"A formal, time-limited exception keeps the risk visible, has an accountable owner accept it and adds compensating controls. Quietly ignoring the standard is tempting because it avoids paperwork, but it hides the risk from governance.","Domain 2: Governance (policy exceptions)",["Silent noncompliance leaves the risk untracked and unapproved by anyone accountable.",null,"Weakening the standard for everyone removes protection from systems that can comply.","Shutting down a business system without weighing the impact may cause more harm than the risk itself."],3],["x85",0,3,"A developer needs to read production application logs to troubleshoot errors but has no need to change anything on the production servers. What access should they receive?",["Full administrator rights on the servers","The same access as the operations team lead","Write access to the application folder","Read-only access to the log location"],3,"Least privilege grants only the access required for the task, which here is reading logs. Copying the team lead's access is tempting because it is quick, but it grants rights beyond the need.","Domain 3: Least privilege",["Administrator rights allow far more than reading logs and violate least privilege.","Copying another person's broader access grants rights the developer does not need.","Write access to the application folder allows changes the task does not require.",null],1],["x86",0,3,"A bank vault can only be opened when two different officers are present, each entering half of the combination. Which control does this illustrate?",["Job rotation","Mandatory vacation","Dual control with split knowledge","Single sign-on"],2,"Dual control requires two people to complete a sensitive action, and split knowledge ensures neither holds the whole secret. Job rotation is tempting because it is also an anti-fraud control, but it works over time rather than at the moment of access.","Domain 3: Separation of duties",["Job rotation moves people between roles over time; it does not require two people at once.","Mandatory vacation forces time away to reveal fraud, not joint action on one task.",null,"Single sign-on lets one login reach many systems, which is the opposite of requiring two people."],2],["x87",0,3,"An access review finds a dozen active accounts belonging to employees who left the company months ago. Which part of the identity lifecycle has failed?",["Provisioning","Deprovisioning","Identification","Authentication"],1,"Deprovisioning removes or disables access when people leave, and orphaned accounts show that step was skipped. Provisioning is tempting because it is the other end of the lifecycle, but it deals with granting access.","Domain 3: Identity lifecycle",["Provisioning creates access for new users; the problem here is access that was never removed.",null,"Identification is a user claiming an identity at login, not the removal of old accounts.","Authentication verifies a claimed identity; it does not remove accounts after people leave."],2],["x88",0,3,"A bank requires employees in sensitive finance roles to take two consecutive weeks off each year while someone else performs their duties. What is the main security purpose?",["To reveal fraud that the absent employee may have been hiding","To reduce the cost of employee overtime","To give the IT team time to patch their workstations","To enforce least privilege on their accounts"],0,"Mandatory vacation puts another person in the role, which can expose ongoing fraud that requires the original employee's constant attention. Least privilege is tempting because it is also an access principle, but it does not rely on absence.","Domain 3: Separation of duties (mandatory vacation)",[null,"Cost savings are not the security goal of this control.","Patching can happen without the employee being away for two weeks.","Least privilege limits access rights; it is not achieved by making someone take leave."],2],["x89",0,3,"A company wants administrators to hold no standing admin rights. Instead, they request elevation for an approved task, receive it for a limited window, and lose it automatically afterward. What is this approach called?",["Just-in-time privileged access","Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Single sign-on"],0,"Just-in-time access, a core privileged access management practice, grants elevated rights only when needed and removes them automatically. RBAC is tempting because admins do have roles, but role permissions are standing rather than temporary.","Domain 3: Privileged access management",[null,"DAC lets resource owners grant access at their discretion, with no time-limited elevation.","RBAC assigns permissions by role, but those permissions usually stay in place permanently.","SSO reduces the number of logins; it does not grant or remove admin rights over time."],3],["x90",0,3,"A company keeps an emergency administrator account whose password is sealed in a safe, used only if normal sign-in systems fail. Which practice best manages the risk of this account?",["Share the password with all administrators by email so it is always at hand during an outage","Disable logging for the account to avoid alert noise","Use it daily so administrators stay familiar with it","Alert on any use, rotate the password after each use and review every session"],3,"A break-glass account is powerful, so every use should trigger alerts, be reviewed and be followed by a password change. Daily use is tempting as a way to keep it working, but it defeats the purpose of an emergency-only account.","Domain 3: Privileged access management",["Sharing the password widely removes control and accountability over a highly privileged account.","Disabling logging hides exactly the use that most needs to be reviewed.","Daily use turns an emergency account into a standing privileged account, increasing risk.",null],3],["x91",0,3,"A company rolls out single sign-on so employees use one login for email, HR and finance apps. Which risk grows as a result, and what control best offsets it?",["Users must remember more passwords, so write them down","Individual apps can no longer log who accessed them, so SSO must be disabled for auditing","One stolen credential can open many apps, so require MFA at the identity provider","Encryption stops working, so switch the apps to plain HTTP"],2,"With SSO, one compromised credential can reach every connected app, so strong MFA at the identity provider is essential. Claiming users must remember more passwords is tempting, but SSO does the opposite.","Domain 3: Single sign-on",["SSO reduces the number of passwords users must remember, not increases it.","Apps and the identity provider can still log access under SSO.",null,"SSO does not break encryption, and moving to plain HTTP would weaken security."],2],["x92",0,3,"Which physical security control can use human judgment to question a visitor, respond to an unexpected situation and call for help?",["A CCTV camera","A security guard","A badge reader","A perimeter fence"],1,"Guards are the physical control that can apply judgment and respond to changing situations. CCTV is tempting because it watches the area, but it only records; a person must still act on what it shows.","Domain 3: Physical access controls",["A camera records or displays activity but cannot question anyone or decide how to respond.",null,"A badge reader follows fixed rules and cannot judge unusual situations.","A fence is a passive barrier with no ability to respond to events."],1],["x93",0,3,"A company requires every visitor to sign a log, wear a visible visitor badge and be escorted by an employee at all times inside the office. What is the main purpose of these measures?",["To encrypt the visitors' data","To speed up visitor entry","To control and track visitor access to the facility","To replace the need for door locks"],2,"Visitor logs, badges and escorts limit where visitors can go and create a record of who was present. Replacing door locks is tempting to pick if you see escorts as a barrier, but these measures add to locks rather than substitute for them.","Domain 3: Physical access controls",["These are physical access measures and have nothing to do with encrypting data.","Sign-in and escort requirements add steps rather than speeding up entry.",null,"Visitor procedures work alongside locks; they do not replace them."],1],["x94",0,3,"In a discretionary access control (DAC) system, a user runs a malicious program that silently shares the user's files with an outsider. Why is DAC especially exposed to this?",["Programs run with the user's rights, and the user as owner can grant access to anyone","DAC systems cannot authenticate users","DAC requires every file to be public","DAC relies on system-assigned classification labels and clearances that malware can easily change"],0,"In DAC, owners control permissions, so malware acting with the owner's rights can change them and share data freely. The label answer is tempting, but labels are a feature of MAC, where users cannot change permissions at will.","Domain 3: Access models (DAC)",[null,"DAC systems do authenticate users; the weakness is in how owners can grant access.","Files in DAC are not public by default; owners decide who gets access.","Classification labels belong to mandatory access control, not DAC."],3],["x95",0,3,"A system locks an account for 15 minutes after five failed login attempts in a row. Which threat is this control mainly designed to slow down?",["Tailgating into the building","Eavesdropping on network traffic","Theft of backup tapes","Password guessing and brute-force attacks"],3,"Account lockout limits how many guesses an attacker can make, which slows brute-force and password guessing. Eavesdropping is tempting because it is also a way to obtain passwords, but lockout does not stop traffic from being captured.","Domain 3: Authentication",["Tailgating is a physical entry threat that account lockout cannot affect.","Eavesdropping captures traffic in transit and is not stopped by limiting login attempts.","Backup theft is a physical media risk unrelated to login attempts.",null],2],["x96",0,3,"During a quarterly access review, who is usually best placed to confirm whether each employee still needs access to a sensitive finance application?",["The help desk technician who originally created the accounts in the directory","The employee's manager or the application's data owner","The employee themselves","An external penetration tester"],1,"Managers and data owners understand the business need, so they are the right people to certify access. The help desk is tempting because it manages the accounts, but it does not decide who needs them.","Domain 3: Periodic access reviews",["The help desk creates accounts on request but does not know each person's current business need.",null,"Employees have a conflict of interest when approving their own access.","A penetration tester looks for technical weaknesses, not whether a person's access is justified."],3],["x97",0,4,"An administrator needs to allow secure remote command-line access to Linux servers through the firewall. Which port should be opened?",["23","22","25","80"],1,"SSH uses TCP port 22 and provides encrypted remote command-line sessions. Port 23 is tempting because Telnet also offers remote command lines, but it is unencrypted.","Domain 4: Common ports",["Port 23 is Telnet, which sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.",null,"Port 25 is SMTP for moving email between servers, not remote administration.","Port 80 is unencrypted HTTP web traffic, not command-line access."],1],["x98",0,4,"A network switch forwards frames to the correct port by reading their MAC addresses. At which OSI layer does this take place?",["Layer 3, network","Layer 4, transport","Layer 1, physical","Layer 2, data link"],3,"MAC addresses and frames belong to the data link layer, where switches operate. Layer 3 is tempting because it also deals with addressing, but it uses IP addresses and routers.","Domain 4: OSI model",["Layer 3 routes packets using IP addresses, not MAC addresses.","Layer 4 handles ports and end-to-end delivery with TCP or UDP.","Layer 1 moves raw bits over the medium and does not read addresses.",null],1],["x99",0,4,"Which of the following IPv4 addresses is a private address that is not routed on the public internet?",["192.168.10.25","8.8.8.8","172.32.1.1","11.0.0.5"],0,"192.168.0.0/16 is one of the private ranges, along with 10.0.0.0/8 and 172.16.0.0/12. 172.32.1.1 is tempting because it looks like the 172.16 private block, but that block ends at 172.31.","Domain 4: IP addressing",[null,"8.8.8.8 is a well-known public address used on the internet.","172.32.1.1 is just outside the private range 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255.","11.0.0.5 is outside the private 10.0.0.0/8 range and is publicly routable."],1],["x100",0,4,"Researchers recover a device's encryption key by carefully measuring tiny changes in its power consumption while it performs cryptographic operations. What type of attack is this?",["Phishing attack","Denial-of-service attack","Side-channel attack","SQL injection"],2,"Side-channel attacks gather secrets from physical effects such as power use, timing or emissions rather than from flaws in the algorithm. Denial of service is tempting because it also targets devices, but it disrupts service instead of extracting data.","Domain 4: Network threats (side-channel)",["Phishing tricks people into revealing information; it does not measure device power use.","A denial-of-service attack aims to make a system unavailable, not to extract keys.",null,"SQL injection targets application databases through crafted input, not hardware signals."],2],["x101",0,4,"Malware spreads from one computer to another across the network on its own, exploiting a vulnerability without any user opening a file or clicking a link. What type of malware is this?",["Trojan","Macro virus","Adware","Worm"],3,"Worms self-propagate across networks without user action, often by exploiting vulnerabilities. A Trojan is tempting because it is also common malware, but it depends on tricking a user into running it.","Domain 4: Network threats (malware)",["A Trojan disguises itself as legitimate software and relies on a user to run it.","A macro virus needs a user to open an infected document before it runs.","Adware shows unwanted ads and does not typically spread across networks by itself.",null],1],["x102",0,4,"A brand-new malware strain has no known signature yet, but it starts encrypting many files rapidly on a laptop. Which antimalware capability is most likely to catch it?",["Signature-based detection","Behavior-based (heuristic) detection","A stateless packet filter","Full-disk encryption"],1,"Behavior-based detection flags suspicious actions such as mass file encryption even when no signature exists. Signature-based detection is tempting because it is the classic antivirus method, but it only recognizes known malware.","Domain 4: Defenses (antivirus)",["Signature detection needs a known pattern, and this new strain has none yet.",null,"A stateless packet filter checks network headers and cannot see file activity on the laptop.","Full-disk encryption protects data if a device is lost but does not detect malware."],2],["x103",0,4,"A real attack passed through the network and compromised a server, but the intrusion detection system raised no alert. What is this outcome called?",["A false positive","A true positive","A false negative","A true negative"],2,"A false negative is when a real attack goes undetected, which is the most dangerous detection failure. A false positive is tempting because it is also an error, but it means an alert on harmless activity.","Domain 4: Defenses (IDS)",["A false positive is an alert for activity that was actually harmless.","A true positive is a correct alert on real malicious activity, and no alert was raised.",null,"A true negative is correctly staying silent when no attack happened."],2],["x104",0,4,"Traffic to a web server is encrypted end to end with TLS, so a network sensor on the segment cannot see inside the requests. Which type of intrusion detection is best placed to inspect the decrypted activity?",["Host-based IDS on the web server","Network-based IDS on the switch span port","A stateless packet filter at the edge","A honeypot elsewhere on the network"],0,"A host-based IDS runs on the server, where traffic has already been decrypted, so it can see the actual requests and system activity. A network-based IDS is tempting because it is the usual traffic sensor, but it cannot read encrypted payloads.","Domain 4: Defenses (IDS/IPS)",[null,"A network sensor sees only encrypted traffic, which is exactly the limitation in this scenario.","A stateless filter checks addresses and ports and cannot inspect the decrypted content.","A honeypot attracts attackers to a decoy; it does not inspect traffic to the real server."],3],["x105",0,4,"A firewall automatically allows the replies to a web request that an internal user started, without a separate inbound rule, because it tracks each connection. What type of firewall is this?",["Stateful firewall","Stateless packet filter","Proxy-only cache","Network hub"],0,"A stateful firewall keeps a table of active connections and permits the matching return traffic automatically. A stateless filter is tempting because it also uses rules, but it does not remember connections.","Domain 4: Defenses (firewalls)",[null,"A stateless filter judges each packet alone, so replies need their own explicit rules.","A caching proxy stores web content; connection tracking is what defines this behavior.","A hub simply repeats traffic to all ports and does no filtering at all."],2],["x106",0,4,"A developer creates a new virtual server through the cloud provider's web portal in a few minutes, without calling or emailing anyone at the provider. Which cloud characteristic does this show?",["Measured service","Resource pooling","On-demand self-service","Community deployment"],2,"On-demand self-service lets customers provision resources themselves whenever they need them. Measured service is tempting because it is another core cloud trait, but it concerns how usage is metered and billed.","Domain 4: Cloud characteristics",["Measured service is about metering and billing usage, not provisioning without help.","Resource pooling is the provider sharing hardware among customers, not self-provisioning.",null,"Community is a deployment model for groups with shared needs, not a service characteristic."],2],["x107",0,4,"Under the shared responsibility model, which task remains the cloud provider's responsibility in IaaS, PaaS and SaaS alike?",["Managing which users can access the customer's data","Physical security of the data centers","Classifying the customer's data","Configuring the customer's user passwords"],1,"The provider always secures the physical facilities, while customers always keep responsibility for their data and user access. Managing user access is tempting because the provider supplies the tools, but using them correctly is the customer's job.","Domain 4: Shared responsibility model",["Deciding who can access customer data remains the customer's responsibility in every model.",null,"Classifying data is always the customer's job, since only they know its value.","Customers manage their own users' credentials and password practices."],2],["x108",0,4,"A web server's connection table fills with thousands of half-open TCP connections from spoofed addresses that never complete the handshake, and legitimate users cannot connect. What attack is this?",["On-path (man-in-the-middle) attack","Side-channel attack","Password spraying","SYN flood"],3,"A SYN flood sends connection requests without completing the handshake, exhausting the server's capacity and denying service. An on-path attack is tempting because it also involves spoofing, but its goal is interception, not exhaustion.","Domain 4: Network threats (DoS)",["An on-path attacker relays and reads traffic between parties rather than exhausting connection tables.","A side-channel attack extracts secrets from physical effects, not by flooding connections.","Password spraying tries common passwords against many accounts; it does not create half-open connections.",null],3],["x109",0,4,"On an office LAN, an attacker sends forged ARP replies claiming that the default gateway's IP address belongs to the attacker's MAC address. What is the attacker most likely trying to achieve?",["Encrypt the victims' files for ransom","Place themselves on-path to intercept traffic headed for the gateway","Guess users' passwords by brute force","Exhaust the DHCP server's address pool so that new devices on the LAN cannot join"],1,"ARP poisoning makes victims send gateway traffic to the attacker, placing the attacker on-path to read or alter it. DHCP exhaustion is tempting because it is another LAN attack, but it works by requesting leases, not by spoofing ARP.","Domain 4: Network threats (on-path, spoofing)",["Ransomware encrypts files on hosts; forged ARP replies redirect network traffic instead.",null,"Brute force repeatedly tries passwords and has nothing to do with ARP replies.","Exhausting DHCP requires many address requests, not forged ARP replies about the gateway."],3],["x110",0,5,"A development team is designing how a new application will store user passwords in its database. What is the best practice?",["Store a salted hash produced by a slow password-hashing algorithm","Store the passwords encrypted with a single strong symmetric key kept on the application server","Store the passwords in plain text in a restricted table","Store the passwords Base64-encoded"],0,"Salted, slow hashes are one-way and make cracking stolen hashes costly, while salts defeat precomputed tables. Encryption is tempting because it protects data, but it is reversible, so one stolen key exposes every password.","Domain 5: Hashing",[null,"Encrypted passwords can all be decrypted if the single key is stolen.","Plain text exposes every password immediately if the table is ever read.","Base64 is an encoding, not protection, and is trivially reversed."],2],["x111",0,5,"Alice wants Bob to be able to verify that a contract really came from her and was not altered. Which key does Alice use to create the digital signature?",["Alice's public key","Bob's public key","Bob's private key","Alice's private key"],3,"A signature is created with the sender's private key and verified with the sender's public key, which proves origin and integrity. Bob's public key is tempting because it is used when sending Bob confidential messages, but that provides secrecy, not a signature.","Domain 5: Encryption (asymmetric, digital signatures)",["Alice's public key is what Bob uses to verify the signature, not to create it.","Encrypting with Bob's public key provides confidentiality for Bob, not proof that Alice sent it.","Alice never has Bob's private key, and using it would prove nothing about Alice.",null],3],["x112",0,5,"Under a company's data classification scheme, into which category would an approved press release that is already posted on the company website normally fall?",["Confidential","Restricted","Public","Internal only"],2,"Information approved for release to anyone is classified as public and needs little protection from disclosure. Internal only is tempting because the release came from inside the company, but it has already been published.","Domain 5: Data classification",["Confidential data would cause harm if disclosed, but this release is meant for everyone.","Restricted is the most sensitive level, for data needing the strongest protection.",null,"Internal-only data is for employees, yet this release has already been published."],1],["x113",0,5,"An attacker who gains administrator rights on a server could delete its local logs to hide their tracks. What is the best way to protect the log evidence?",["Keep logs only on the local server's disk","Turn off logging to save storage space","Forward logs in real time to a central log server or SIEM that the server's admins cannot modify","Print the logs once a month"],2,"Sending logs off the host in real time keeps a protected copy even if the server is compromised. Keeping logs locally is tempting because it is the default, but local admins, including an attacker, can erase them.","Domain 5: Logging and monitoring",["Local logs are exactly what an attacker with admin rights can delete.","Turning off logging removes the evidence entirely instead of protecting it.",null,"Monthly printouts leave weeks of activity unprotected and are hard to search."],2],["x114",0,5,"A critical operating system patch has just been released. Before deploying it to all production servers, what should normally happen first?",["Deploy it to every server at once to save time","Wait a year to see whether other companies have problems","Uninstall the antivirus to avoid conflicts","Test the patch in a non-production environment"],3,"Testing patches first catches compatibility problems before they hit production, as part of patch and change management. Deploying everywhere at once is tempting because the patch is critical, but an untested patch can cause its own outage.","Domain 5: Patching",["Deploying everywhere at once risks a widespread outage if the patch causes problems.","Waiting a year leaves a critical vulnerability open for far too long.","Removing antivirus weakens protection and is not part of normal patch testing.",null],1],["x115",0,5,"A tool compares each server's current security settings with an approved secure configuration and reports any server that has drifted. Which practice does this support?",["Configuration management against a baseline","Business impact analysis","Data classification","Penetration testing"],0,"Configuration management keeps systems aligned with an approved baseline and flags unauthorized drift. Penetration testing is tempting because it also finds weaknesses, but it simulates attacks instead of comparing settings.","Domain 5: Configuration management",[null,"A BIA ranks business functions by impact, not server settings.","Data classification labels information by sensitivity, not system settings.","Penetration testing actively tries to exploit weaknesses rather than checking settings against a standard."],2],["x116",0,5,"With written authorization, a team actively tries to exploit weaknesses in a company's web application to show what a real attacker could achieve. What is this activity?",["Vulnerability scanning","Penetration testing","Log review","Business impact analysis"],1,"Penetration testing goes beyond finding weaknesses by safely exploiting them to demonstrate real impact. Vulnerability scanning is tempting because it also looks for weaknesses, but it stops at identifying them.","Domain 5: Security testing",["A vulnerability scan identifies likely weaknesses but does not try to exploit them.",null,"Log review examines records of past activity rather than testing defenses.","A BIA evaluates the impact of losing business functions, not technical weaknesses."],2],["x117",0,5,"An analyst confirms that an alert reflects a real compromise and works to determine which systems and accounts are affected. Which incident response phase is this?",["Preparation","Eradication","Lessons learned","Detection and analysis"],3,"Confirming an incident and scoping its impact belong to detection and analysis. Eradication is tempting because the analyst is working on the compromise, but removal happens only after scoping and containment.","Domain 5: Incident response lifecycle",["Preparation happens before incidents, building plans, tools and training.","Eradication removes the threat after its scope is understood and it has been contained.","Lessons learned is the review held after the incident is resolved.",null],2],["x118",0,5,"A company takes a full backup every Sunday and a differential backup every other night. A server fails on Thursday morning. Which backups are needed to restore the latest data?",["Sunday's full backup and every differential from Monday to Wednesday","Only Wednesday night's differential","Sunday's full backup and Wednesday night's differential","Sunday's full backup only"],2,"A differential holds every change since the last full backup, so the full plus the most recent differential restores the latest data. Restoring every differential is tempting, but that is how incremental backups work, not differentials.","Domain 5: Backups",["Each differential already includes all changes since Sunday, so the earlier ones are not needed.","A differential only contains changes since the full backup, so it cannot be restored by itself.",null,"The full backup alone would lose all changes made from Monday to Wednesday."],3],["x119",0,5,"A company worries that ransomware could encrypt its backups along with its production data. Which backup practice best protects against this?",["Store every backup on a share mapped to all user PCs","Keep at least one backup copy offline or immutable","Back up only once a year to reduce exposure","Keep backups on the same server as the data"],1,"An offline or immutable copy cannot be changed by ransomware running on the network, so it remains available for recovery. Keeping backups on the same server is tempting for convenience, but it shares the same fate as the data.","Domain 5: Backups",["A share mapped to every PC is exactly where ransomware can reach and encrypt the backups.",null,"Yearly backups would lose up to a year of data and do not stop encryption of the backups.","Backups on the same server fall with it when ransomware encrypts that server."],2],["x120",0,5,"New employees must read and sign a document describing what they may and may not do with company computers, email and internet access. What is this document?",["Acceptable use policy (AUP)","Business continuity plan","Service level agreement","Disaster recovery plan"],0,"An AUP defines acceptable and prohibited use of company resources, and employees acknowledge it by signing. An SLA is tempting because it is also a signed agreement, but it covers service levels rather than user behavior.","Domain 5: Best-practice policies (AUP)",[null,"A business continuity plan explains how to keep operating during a disruption, not user conduct.","An SLA sets service commitments between a provider and customer, not employee behavior.","A disaster recovery plan covers restoring IT after a disaster, not everyday system use."],1]]);
